  "notes": [
    "This is a forecast, not a measurement. It uses only the farm's manual DailyLog egg counts up to 2026-09-13 (the last day with eggs logged). Camera egg counts are not used.",
    "Accuracy, stated plainly: in the latest backtest (cutoff 2026-08-16) the model's average error was 3.07 points. It beat the breed-standard baseline (4.61) and did not beat the simple last-7-day mean (1.48). The model under-predicted by 2.9 points on average in that window. Lay has been flat for months, so a recent average is hard to beat over 28 days. The model's value is the breed-curve context, the interval and the driver breakdown, not better accuracy.",
    "HPED = (small + medium + large + XL + jumbo) / live hens × 100, the same formula as the app (damaged eggs are not counted in the total). Live hens = 4,479 starting hens minus cumulative DailyLog mortality.",
    "The model was trained on 288 logged days from 2025-11-10 to 2026-09-13. Shed sensors have no data before 2026-02-20 or from 2026-02-26 to 2026-04-13. On those days THI was set to its average, so it adds nothing there. In total 334 DailyLog days with eggs exist since first eggs on 2025-10-13.",
    "The first 28 days of lay (to 2025-11-09) are excluded from fitting. Other excluded days, kept in the history and flagged: 7 days below 50%, a logging gap or partial count: 2025-12-01 (27.5%), 2025-12-02 (3.1%), 2025-12-03 (2.8%), 2025-12-04 (0.4%), 2025-12-05 (2.2%), 2025-12-06 (2.7%), 2025-12-07 (4.3%); 11 days above 100% of live hens, likely eggs carried over from another day: 2025-11-16 (103.6%), 2025-11-20 (101.6%), 2025-11-23 (104.0%), 2025-11-24 (109.4%), 2025-12-16 (101.9%), 2025-12-20 (108.2%), 2025-12-31 (104.8%), 2026-01-29 (104.4%), 2026-04-04 (101.2%), 2026-04-17 (101.4%), 2026-05-13 (100.3%); 1 day a single-day count more than 15 points below the days around it, likely a partial count: 2026-06-30 (69.1%).",
    "Dates with no DailyLog row: 2026-06-22.",
    "Damaged eggs were logged as 0 on 8 laying days (2025-11-10, 2026-02-24, 2026-06-25, 2026-07-20, 2026-07-28, 2026-07-29, 2026-08-04, 2026-09-07). These are treated as not recorded, and the cracked % regressor carries the previous day's value forward.",
    "All regressors are lagged by one day so they are known when the forecast is made. Over the forecast, shed THI uses actual sensor readings where they exist (25 of 28 forecast days; sensors run to 2026-10-07) and the mean of the last 14 sensor days otherwise. Cracked % and size mix are held at their last-14-day means.",
    "Shed THI never reached the 27.8 °C-eq heat-stress level in the training period (maximum daily peak 26.0). The THI effects therefore describe cooler versus milder days, not heat stress, so the model cannot yet say how a hot spell would affect lay.",
    "Driver contributions come straight from the model's additive components, so SHAP is not needed. Each value is measured against that driver's average level in the training period, so 0 means an average day.",
    "For recent drops, 'expected' is the model baseline for that day: the anchor, trend and weekday pattern with every driver at its average. drop_pts = actual − expected. It splits into the listed driver contributions plus the part the model does not explain.",
    "Model choice: 24 variants (target, anchor, trend, training window) were scored on two earlier 28-day backtests (cutoffs 2026-06-21 and 2026-07-19). The reported backtest (cutoff 2026-08-16) was not used for the choice. It is a single 28-day window, so treat the error as indicative.",
    "The breed standard (src/lib/henStandard.ts) is a generic layer curve, not this breed's exact curve. This flock has run well above it (recent gap about 8.3 points).",
    "The 80% interval reflects day-to-day noise and trend uncertainty seen in training. It does not cover events the model has not seen, such as disease, a feed change, a lighting change or a counting error.",
    "The cracked-egg effect rests mostly on one event. Cracked eggs reached 7.2% on 2026-03-10 and lay fell to 65.2% the next day. Read its contribution as indicative only.",
    "There is only one autumn-winter-spring of climate data, so the THI effect is mixed up with season (for example day length and the flock's age). Lay dipped in midwinter and recovered as the shed warmed. The model credits that recovery to milder THI, but this is an association, not a proven cause."
  ]
